From: Cargt <tim.carney@cargt.com>
Subject: [PATCH] resize-helper: extend the userfs GPT partition before
 growing it

On the SD card build, userfs is created as a small fixed-size GPT
partition (200M - see the sdcard .wks.in), with the rest of the card
left genuinely unpartitioned. resize2fs and systemd-growfs both only
ever grow a filesystem to fill its *existing* partition boundary -
neither one ever touches the partition table itself - so without an
explicit partition-table extension first, neither has anything to grow
into and userfs stays stuck at 200M forever, no matter how many times
this script (or init-resize.sh) runs or how successfully it reports.

eMMC doesn't hit this: its flashlayout sizes userfs to consume the
whole remaining capacity directly at flash time, so there's normally
no free space left after it for this new code to find or need to act
on - a safe no-op there.

Confirmed on hardware: after writing a freshly-built SD card image to
both DUTs and booting, userfs was stuck at 200M on both despite
RESIZE-HELPER reporting "FINISH" and systemd-growfs completing without
error - `df -h` showed the fixed 200M size, and sgdisk showed the
userfs partition table entry itself unchanged, with ~22GB of genuinely
unpartitioned space sitting after it on the disk.

Fix: before calling systemd-growfs/resize2fs, extend the userfs GPT
partition's table entry to consume the rest of the disk (delete and
recreate it with the same start sector, type GUID, unique GUID and
name - so nothing about how the partition is identified changes, only
where it ends), then tell the running kernel about the new size via
resizepart (falling back to partprobe). This is guarded so it only
ever acts when userfs is genuinely the last partition on the disk and
there's a meaningful amount of real free space after it - both to
avoid colliding with another partition on some other unforeseen
layout, and so it's a safe no-op on the already-correctly-sized eMMC
case. Also handles the disk-image-dd'd-onto-a-larger-card gotcha: the
GPT backup header (and the primary header's own recorded "last usable
sector") still reflect the original, smaller built image size until
`sgdisk -e` runs once - without that, the free space after the last
partition isn't visible to sgdisk at all and the extend silently does
nothing.

The device this function is called with is resolved from the root
filesystem's own disk (mmcblk0 vs mmcblk1), not from /usr/local's
current mount state: this call site runs right after this same script
may have already called mount-partitions.sh stop (userfs is in its
MOUNT_PARTITIONS_LIST), which would make a findmnt-on-/usr/local
lookup here return nothing despite the device still existing. Uses
`lsblk -l` (not the tree-drawing default) so the device name isn't
prefixed with a tree-branch character when listing a whole disk's
partitions.

This function's own logic was verified working correctly on hardware
independent of the rest of this change (manually re-invoked twice,
both times growing userfs from 200M to ~21GB correctly) before a
separate, unrelated bug elsewhere was found and fixed: see
0005-dont-mark-userfs-resized-from-the-initrd.patch in the
initrd-boot recipe - init-resize.sh, which always runs before this
script, was unconditionally marking userfs "done" after its own
resize2fs call, regardless of whether that call actually grew
anything (it can't, on SD card, without the extend logic added here) -
which caused this script's entire resize block, including this
function, to be skipped via the shared marker before it ever got a
chance to run. Confirmed fixed together on hardware: userfs now grows
from 200M to ~21GB automatically on a fresh SD card's first boot, no
manual intervention.

Upstream-Status: Pending [not sent upstream to ST - specific to this
project's A/B partition scheme's SD card sizing]

Signed-off-by: Cargt <tim.carney@cargt.com>
---
diff --git a/resize-helper b/resize-helper
index 1111111111..2222222222 100644
--- a/resize-helper
+++ b/resize-helper
@@ -43,6 +43,78 @@ USERFS_RESIZE_MARKER=/usr/local/.resize-helper-done
 USERFS_ALREADY_RESIZED=0
 [ -f "$USERFS_RESIZE_MARKER" ] && USERFS_ALREADY_RESIZED=1

+# On the SD card build, userfs is created as a small fixed-size GPT
+# partition (200M, see the .wks.in) with the rest of the card left
+# genuinely unpartitioned - resize2fs/systemd-growfs only ever grow a
+# filesystem to fill its *existing* partition boundary, they never touch
+# the partition table itself, so without this neither one has anything
+# to grow into and userfs stays stuck at 200M forever. (eMMC doesn't hit
+# this: its flashlayout sizes userfs to consume the whole remaining
+# capacity directly at flash time, so there's normally no free space
+# after it for this to find or need to act on - a safe no-op there.)
+#
+# Confirmed on hardware: after writing today's SD card image and
+# booting, userfs was stuck at 200M despite both the initrd-stage and
+# this script's own resize attempts completing "successfully" - because
+# both were only ever resizing the filesystem within an unchanged 200M
+# partition. Extending the GPT partition first (guarded to only ever do
+# so when this is genuinely the last partition on the disk with real
+# free space after it, so it can't collide with a following partition
+# or fire pointlessly on a normal, already-correctly-sized eMMC) fixed
+# it: verified on both DUTs' SD cards, userfs grew from 200M to ~21GB.
+extend_partition_if_last_and_free_space() {
+    device="$1"
+    disk="/dev/$(lsblk -lno PKNAME "$device" 2>/dev/null)"
+    partnum=$(cat "/sys/class/block/$(basename "$device")/partition" 2>/dev/null)
+    [ -n "$disk" ] && [ -n "$partnum" ] || return 0
+    [ -f /usr/sbin/sgdisk ] || return 0
+
+    # Only proceed if $device is the last partition on $disk and there's
+    # real free space after it - otherwise this is either not our
+    # situation (already fills the disk, e.g. eMMC) or would be unsafe
+    # (extending anything but the true last partition risks colliding
+    # with whatever comes after it).
+    last_part_line=$(sgdisk -p "$disk" 2>/dev/null | awk '$1 ~ /^[0-9]+$/{line=$0} END{print line}')
+    last_partnum=$(echo "$last_part_line" | awk '{print $1}')
+    [ "$last_partnum" = "$partnum" ] || return 0
+
+    end_sector=$(echo "$last_part_line" | awk '{print $3}')
+    # sgdisk -e first: on an image dd'd onto a larger physical disk than
+    # it was built for, the backup GPT header (and the primary header's
+    # own recorded "last usable sector") still reflect the original,
+    # smaller image size until this runs once - without it, the free
+    # space after the last partition isn't visible to sgdisk at all, and
+    # the extend below silently does nothing.
+    sgdisk -e "$disk" >/dev/null 2>&1
+    last_usable=$(sgdisk -p "$disk" 2>/dev/null | sed -n 's/.*last usable sector is \([0-9]*\).*/\1/p')
+    [ -n "$last_usable" ] || return 0
+    # Require a real, meaningful amount of free space (>16MiB) before
+    # bothering - a few sectors of alignment slack isn't worth acting on.
+    free_sectors=$((last_usable - end_sector))
+    [ "$free_sectors" -gt 32768 ] || return 0
+
+    echo "Extending $device ($disk partition $partnum) to use $free_sectors free sectors" > /dev/kmsg
+    was_mounted=$(findmnt --noheadings --output=TARGET "$device" 2>/dev/null)
+    [ -n "$was_mounted" ] && umount "$device"
+
+    start_sector=$(sgdisk -i "$partnum" "$disk" 2>/dev/null | sed -n 's/^First sector: \([0-9]*\).*/\1/p')
+    type_guid=$(sgdisk -i "$partnum" "$disk" 2>/dev/null | sed -n 's/^Partition GUID code: \([0-9A-Fa-f-]*\).*/\1/p')
+    unique_guid=$(sgdisk -i "$partnum" "$disk" 2>/dev/null | sed -n "s/^Partition unique GUID: \([0-9A-Fa-f-]*\).*/\1/p")
+    part_name=$(sgdisk -i "$partnum" "$disk" 2>/dev/null | sed -n "s/^Partition name: '\(.*\)'$/\1/p")
+
+    if [ -n "$start_sector" ] && [ -n "$type_guid" ] && [ -n "$unique_guid" ]; then
+        sgdisk -d "$partnum" \
+               -n "${partnum}:${start_sector}:0" \
+               -t "${partnum}:${type_guid}" \
+               -c "${partnum}:${part_name}" \
+               -u "${partnum}:${unique_guid}" \
+               "$disk"
+        resizepart "$disk" "$partnum" $((last_usable - start_sector + 1)) 2>/dev/null || partprobe "$disk" 2>/dev/null
+    fi
+
+    [ -n "$was_mounted" ] && mount "$device" "$was_mounted"
+}
+
 echo "RESIZE-HELPER START" > /dev/kmsg

 # disable service to be sure it will be executed only one time
@@ -65,6 +137,24 @@ if [ -f /lib/systemd/systemd-growfs ]; then
         [ -f /sbin/mount-partitions.sh ] && /sbin/mount-partitions.sh stop
     fi
     if [ "$USERFS_ALREADY_RESIZED" = "0" ]; then
+        # Resolve via the root filesystem's own disk, not via /usr/local's
+        # current mount state - mount-partitions.sh stop, just above, may
+        # have already unmounted /usr/local by this point (it's in
+        # MOUNT_PARTITIONS_LIST), which would make a findmnt-on-/usr/local
+        # lookup here return nothing despite the device still existing.
+        root_device=$(findmnt --noheadings --output=SOURCE / 2>/dev/null | cut -d'[' -f1)
+        mmc_disk=""
+        case "$root_device" in
+            /dev/mmcblk0*) mmc_disk=/dev/mmcblk0 ;;
+            /dev/mmcblk1*) mmc_disk=/dev/mmcblk1 ;;
+            /dev/mmcblk2*) mmc_disk=/dev/mmcblk2 ;;
+        esac
+        userfs_device=""
+        if [ -n "$mmc_disk" ]; then
+            userfs_part=$(lsblk -lno NAME,PARTLABEL "$mmc_disk" 2>/dev/null | awk '$2=="userfs"{print $1}')
+            [ -n "$userfs_part" ] && userfs_device="/dev/${userfs_part}"
+        fi
+        [ -n "$userfs_device" ] && extend_partition_if_last_and_free_space "$userfs_device"
         /lib/systemd/systemd-growfs /usr/local/
         touch "$USERFS_RESIZE_MARKER"
     fi
@@ -79,6 +169,7 @@ else
                 PART_LABEL=$(udevadm info --query=property --name=/dev/${device} | grep '^ID_PART_ENTRY_NAME=' | cut -d'=' -f2)
                 if [ "$PART_LABEL" = "userfs" ] && [ "$USERFS_ALREADY_RESIZED" = "0" ]; then
                     echo "Resize /dev/${device}"
+                    extend_partition_if_last_and_free_space "/dev/${device}"
                     e2fsck -f -y -c -C 0 "/dev/${device}"
                     ${RESIZE2FS} "/dev/${device}"
                     mkdir -p /tmp/userfs-mark
